            <ArticleTitle>Initial results with a mini-posterior approach for total hip arthroplasty.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>S17-20</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Implantation of the total hip prosthesis should always be as atraumatic as possible. The principle underlying this technique is to visualise the working area while keeping the aperture to a minimum, with a resulting reduction in trauma to the soft tissue. We present a new, improved single-incision approach in total hip arthroplasty: the mini-posterior approach. Preliminary results from 60 patients operated using this approach indicate rapid functional recovery, minimal postoperative pain, a reduced duration of hospitalisation, few complications, and optimal component positioning. Further follow-up is warranted.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
